// Where the monitor is: every connector of a Polaris card (DCE 11.2)
// looked at, at boot and again and again while brixOS runs, and a record
// of each kept for whoever acts on it. Nobody chooses the output: the
// card's ROM names the connectors (gpu_rom.mlx), each connector's hot
// plug line says whether something is plugged in, the encoder blocks say
// by which connector the firmware's picture goes out and what it sends,
// and one line of the log says what was found and which path follows.
// The path goes by what the output sends, not by the connector's kind: a
// DisplayPort connector whose back end sends TMDS (a passive adapter to
// HDMI or DVI behind it) is on the TMDS path.
//
// LOOKING WRITES NOTHING. Everything up to "THE MONITOR ASKED" below
// reaches the card through gpu_display's `Card`, which can only read a
// register: so it is done at every boot, under the choice that keeps the
// firmware's picture too, and from the timer's tick
// (tests/483_gpu_plug_detect_runtime.mlx counts the writes: none).
//
// A HOT PLUG LINE, as Linux 6.6 reads it on this card. The ROM's hot plug
// record of a connector names a pin, the ROM's table of pins places it on
// one of the card's six lines (gpu_rom.mlx's `lineOf`: the register
// DC_GPIO_HPD_A and the line's bit in it; dc/gpio/dce110/hw_translate_
// dce110.c's offset_to_id). Line n (1 to 6) has a block of registers,
// eight numbers apart (HPD0_ to HPD5_ in dce_11_2_d.h): the display core
// reads DC_HPD_INT_STATUS's DC_HPD_SENSE_DELAYED, the line behind the
// block's filter (dc/gpio/hw_hpd.c's get_value, by way of
// dc/link/protocols/link_hpd.c's query_hpd_status and link_detection.c's
// link_detect_connection_type: high, something is there; low, nothing);
// the older amdgpu reads DC_HPD_SENSE, the line as it is (amdgpu/
// dce_v11_0.c's dce_v11_0_hpd_sense). Here the word is read once and
// both bits are taken: when they agree that is the line's level, when
// they differ the line is changing and is looked at again.
// What that reading rests on, each read and said when it does not stand:
// - the block is on: DC_HPD_CONTROL's DC_HPD_EN. The display core never
//   sets the bit (the one function that would, dce110_link_encoder_
//   enable_hpd, changes a copy of the word and writes nothing): it
//   counts on the firmware having left it on. The older amdgpu sets it
//   at its start (dce_v11_0_hpd_init).
// - the pin is the block's: its bit in DC_GPIO_HPD_MASK is 0. The display
//   core writes that 0 before it looks (hw_gpio.c's
//   dal_hw_gpio_config_mode for the interrupt mode).
// A line whose block is off or whose pin is held as a plain pin is not
// judged: "not known". Setting either is a write, and not made here.
// The block's filter (DC_HPD_TOGGLE_FILT_CNTL) is read and said only:
// Linux writes it (program_hpd_filter: a plug on an HDMI or DVI connector
// counts after 500 ms, an unplug after 100 ms; on a DisplayPort connector
// 80 ms and at once), this driver takes those times for its own looks
// below and leaves the word as the firmware has it. (Its two delays are
// said as the numbers the word holds, without a unit: Linux's display
// core writes 50 and 10 there and means 500 and 100 ms, a tenth of the
// milliseconds, hw_hpd.c's set_config; the older amdgpu writes the same
// 50 and 10 and calls them milliseconds, amdgpu_mode.h's
// AMDGPU_HPD_CONNECT_INT_DELAY_IN_MS and dce_v11_0_hpd_init.)
//
// BY WHICH CONNECTOR THE PICTURE GOES OUT (`serve`): a connector's
// transmitter is a back end (the DIG block of that number); the picture
// goes out there when the back end is on and sends a front end whose
// source is a controller that runs (the words gpu_display.mlx's read-out
// goes by). Its mode says what it sends.
//
// THE DETECTION (`detect`, `sayVerdict`), exactly these cases, each with
// its line: the firmware's output has a monitor (the usual one: the plan
// is for it); it has one and other connectors have something too; it has
// none and another connector has (that one is not brought up yet); the
// line of the firmware's output cannot be judged (the plan goes on as
// the firmware left it); nothing is plugged in anywhere; the firmware
// started no output of this card; the ROM has no connector records.
// Linux sets no mode on an output whose line is low (no sink is made
// for it: detect_link_and_local_sink), so the plan is not carried out
// when the line of the output it is for says, for certain, that nothing
// is plugged in (`emptyFor`; gpu.mlx asks before it lets gpu_set.mlx
// write). "For certain": the block on, the pin the block's, both sense
// bits low. Whatever is less than that stops nothing.
//
// WHILE brixOS RUNS (`look`): every INTERVAL the lines are read again,
// two registers a line and one for all. A change is taken when AGREE
// looks in a row saw it and it has lasted as long as Linux's filter
// would wait for that kind of connector and direction (above; a
// DisplayPort's 80 ms and 0 come to the two looks). So a pulse shorter
// than two looks is not seen as a change, and a line that bounces is
// taken when it rests. A line whose two sense bits differ at every look
// for longer than a filter of the block's own could hold them apart
// (`settle`: BOOT_SETTLE at least; the delayed one does not follow) is
// taken as one that cannot be read any more, and said, as it is at
// boot: the record never keeps a level the line no longer says. Each
// change is given to the caller once (`Changes`), with the connector's
// record brought up to date.
//
// THE MONITOR ASKED (`askAtBoot`, `tick`): on a connector that sends or
// would send TMDS the monitor's EDID is read over the connector's DDC
// line by gpu_ddc.mlx, which writes registers of the card's I2C engine
// (and puts them back): only with the `Hands` gpu.mlx lends, and it
// lends none at a boot that keeps the firmware's picture. At boot: the
// firmware's output unless its line says for certain that nothing is
// there, and every other HDMI or DVI connector whose line is high. While
// brixOS runs: when something is plugged into such a connector; a
// monitor that does not answer is asked once more a second later (the
// older amdgpu's rule for a plug whose DDC pins make contact after the
// hot plug pin: amdgpu_connector_dvi_detect). The reading is a piece of
// work a tick (gpu_ddc.mlx's `Reader.step`), never a wait in the
// timer's interrupt.
// The EDID read from the firmware's output is held against the one the
// EFI loader got from the firmware (`counts`): the same as far as both
// go, the one that has more of it is what the plan is made from (a
// firmware may give the loader the first block alone, and the block
// that says a monitor is an HDMI one is the second); another one, the
// driver's own when it was read whole; nothing read, the loader's.
// Not asked: a DisplayPort connector that runs DisplayPort (its AUX
// channel is another file's), and one the firmware did not start: what
// is plugged into it, a monitor or an adapter, Linux tells by a pin of
// the DDC line it has to switch for that (dp_is_sink_present), which is
// the next stage's.
//
// FOR WHAT ACTS ON A CHANGE: `Ports` is the record, a `Port` each
// connector: what the ROM says, the line's level and since when, what
// serves it now, the EDID when it was read. Every function here can be
// called again at any time: none waits, none counts on being the only
// thing that runs but for this: one EDID is read at a time on the whole
// card (`Watch.reading`), and nothing else may use the I2C engine or a
// DDC line's pins meanwhile.
const scanout = @import("./gpu_display.mlx")
const videorom = @import("./gpu_rom.mlx")
const atom = @import("./atom.mlx")
const edid = @import("./edid.mlx")
const ddc = @import("./gpu_ddc.mlx")

pub const NONE: usize = 0xFFFFFFFF
pub const LINES: usize = 6

// A hot plug line's block (line 1's numbers; HPD_STEP apart) and the
// word that says which of the six pins the blocks have.
pub const DC_HPD_INT_STATUS: usize = 0x1898
pub const DC_HPD_CONTROL: usize = 0x189A
pub const DC_HPD_TOGGLE_FILT_CNTL: usize = 0x189C
pub const DC_GPIO_HPD_MASK: usize = 0x488C
pub const HPD_STEP: usize = 8
pub const DC_HPD_INT_STATUS__DC_HPD_SENSE: usize = 0x2
pub const DC_HPD_INT_STATUS__DC_HPD_SENSE_DELAYED: usize = 0x10
pub const DC_HPD_CONTROL__DC_HPD_EN: usize = 0x10000000
pub const DC_HPD_TOGGLE_FILT_CNTL__DC_HPD_CONNECT_INT_DELAY: usize = 0xFF
pub const DC_HPD_TOGGLE_FILT_CNTL__DC_HPD_DISCONNECT_INT_DELAY: usize = 0xFF00000
// Each line's pin in DC_GPIO_HPD_MASK (DC_GPIO_HPD1_MASK to
// DC_GPIO_HPD6_MASK: not the bits the pins have in DC_GPIO_HPD_A).
pub const HPD_PIN_MASKS = [_]usize{ 0x1, 0x100, 0x10000, 0x100000, 0x1000000, 0x10000000 }

// Linux's filter times in milliseconds (program_hpd_filter).
pub const CONNECT_TMDS: usize = 500
pub const DISCONNECT_TMDS: usize = 100
pub const CONNECT_DP: usize = 80
pub const DISCONNECT_DP: usize = 0
// Ours, in nanoseconds: from one look to the next; how long a line that
// is changing at boot is looked at before it is left as "changing"
// (longer than the longest filter); when a monitor that did not answer
// is asked again. And how many looks have to agree, and how many pieces
// of an EDID's reading a tick does at most.
pub const INTERVAL: usize = 100000000
pub const BOOT_SETTLE: usize = 600000000
pub const ASK_AGAIN: usize = 1000000000
pub const AGREE: usize = 2
pub const PIECES: usize = 16
// (And a count for the boot's settling, should the clock stand still:
// so many looks at a changing line and no more.)
pub const BOOT_LOOKS: usize = 2000000

// What a hot plug line says.
pub const Level = enum(u8) {
    // The connector has no line the card reads.
    no_line,
    // What the reading rests on does not stand (`Why`).
    not_known,
    // The line's two sense bits differ.
    changing,
    low,
    high
}

pub const Why = enum(u8) {
    judged,
    // The ROM names no hot plug pin for the connector; names one that is
    // none of the card's six lines.
    no_record,
    not_placed,
    // The block's words read as all ones; the block is off; the pin is
    // held as a plain pin.
    no_answer,
    block_off,
    pin_masked
}

// What a connector's back end sends.
pub const Sends = enum(u8) { nothing, tmds, displayport, other }

// One connector.
pub const Port = struct {
    // Its number in the ROM's list (the log's "connector N"), what the
    // ROM says of it, and its DDC line worked out from that.
    index: usize
    rom: videorom.Connector
    wire: ddc.Wire
    // Its hot plug line (1 to 6; 0: none). The line's level as taken
    // (after the looks agreed), why it is not judged when it is not,
    // since when it is so (the clock's nanoseconds) and whether that is
    // how it was found at boot.
    line: usize
    level: Level
    why: Why
    since: usize
    atBoot: bool
    // What the last look saw, since when, in how many looks in a row.
    seen: Level
    seenSince: usize
    seenLooks: usize
    // The line's words as last read (the filter's at the survey only).
    status: usize
    control: usize
    filter: usize
    masked: bool
    // What serves it now: whether a picture goes out here, the
    // controller, the front end, the back end's mode (gpu_display's
    // MODE_), and what that sends. The back end is the ROM's transmitter.
    firmware: bool
    controller: usize
    frontEnd: usize
    mode: usize
    sends: Sends
    // The monitor's EDID as the driver read it (not_asked: it did not),
    // when, and what it says. While brixOS runs: when the monitor is to
    // be asked (0: it is not) and how often it was.
    heard: ddc.Heard
    heardAt: usize
    monitor: edid.Monitor
    offer: edid.Offer
    askAt: usize
    asked: usize

    // Whether the level is one a line was read as (not "none", "not
    // known" or "changing").
    pub fn judged(self: *const Self) -> bool { return self.*.level == Level.low || self.*.level == Level.high }

    // Whether the connector is one a TMDS signal leaves by as it is
    // (HDMI, DVI).
    pub fn tmdsKind(self: *const Self) -> bool {
        const kind = self.*.rom.kind
        return kind == videorom.CONNECTOR_OBJECT_ID_HDMI_TYPE_A || kind == videorom.CONNECTOR_OBJECT_ID_HDMI_TYPE_B || kind == videorom.CONNECTOR_OBJECT_ID_SINGLE_LINK_DVI_I || kind == videorom.CONNECTOR_OBJECT_ID_DUAL_LINK_DVI_I || kind == videorom.CONNECTOR_OBJECT_ID_SINGLE_LINK_DVI_D || kind == videorom.CONNECTOR_OBJECT_ID_DUAL_LINK_DVI_D
    }

    // Whether its monitor is one to ask over the DDC line: an HDMI or
    // DVI connector, or whatever connector sends TMDS now.
    pub fn asks(self: *const Self) -> bool { return self.tmdsKind() || (self.*.firmware && self.*.sends == Sends.tmds) }
}

pub const Ports = struct {
    // Whether the ROM's connector records were read and name any.
    found: bool
    count: usize
    ports: [videorom.CONNECTORS]Port
    // How many looks were made, and when the last one.
    looks: usize
    lookedAt: usize
}

// A line's level after `look` took a change.
pub const Change = struct {
    port: usize
    was: Level
    now: Level
}

pub const Changes = struct {
    count: usize
    list: [videorom.CONNECTORS]Change
}

// The back end `block`'s words read: whether a picture goes out by it.
pub fn serve(card: *const scanout.Card, port: *Port) -> void {
    port.*.firmware = false
    port.*.controller = NONE
    port.*.frontEnd = NONE
    port.*.mode = 0
    port.*.sends = Sends.nothing
    const back = port.*.rom.transmitter
    if back >= scanout.BACK_ENDS { return }
    const enabled = card.reg(scanout.DIG[back] + scanout.DIG_BE_EN_CNTL)
    const control = card.reg(scanout.DIG[back] + scanout.DIG_BE_CNTL)
    if enabled == 0xFFFFFFFF || enabled & 1 == 0 || control == 0xFFFFFFFF { return }
    var front: usize = 0
    while front < scanout.FRONT_ENDS {
        if (control >> @intCast(u6, 8 + front)) & 1 != 0 {
            const source = card.reg(scanout.DIG[front] + scanout.DIG_FE_CNTL) & 7
            if source < scanout.CONTROLLERS {
                const running = card.reg(scanout.OFFSETS[source] + scanout.CRTC_CONTROL)
                if running != 0xFFFFFFFF && running & 1 != 0 {
                    port.*.firmware = true
                    port.*.controller = source
                    port.*.frontEnd = front
                    port.*.mode = (control >> 16) & 7
                    port.*.sends = if port.*.mode == scanout.MODE_DVI || port.*.mode == scanout.MODE_HDMI { Sends.tmds } else if scanout.isDisplayPort(port.*.mode) { Sends.displayport } else { Sends.other }
                    return
                }
            }
        }
        front += 1
    }
}

// What serves each connector read anew (after a mode was set, or an
// output brought up or taken down).
pub fn serveAll(card: *const scanout.Card, ports: *Ports) -> void {
    var k: usize = 0
    while k < ports.*.count {
        serve(card, &ports.*.ports[k])
        k += 1
    }
}

// A line read once: its words kept in the record, the level they say.
// `pins`: DC_GPIO_HPD_MASK as read for this look (one word for all
// lines).
fn readLine(card: *const scanout.Card, port: *Port, pins: usize) -> Level {
    if port.*.line == 0 { return Level.no_line }
    const at = (port.*.line - 1) * HPD_STEP
    port.*.status = card.reg(DC_HPD_INT_STATUS + at)
    port.*.control = card.reg(DC_HPD_CONTROL + at)
    port.*.masked = pins & HPD_PIN_MASKS[port.*.line - 1] != 0
    if port.*.status == 0xFFFFFFFF || port.*.control == 0xFFFFFFFF || pins == 0xFFFFFFFF {
        port.*.why = Why.no_answer
        return Level.not_known
    }
    if port.*.control & DC_HPD_CONTROL__DC_HPD_EN == 0 {
        port.*.why = Why.block_off
        return Level.not_known
    }
    if port.*.masked {
        port.*.why = Why.pin_masked
        return Level.not_known
    }
    port.*.why = Why.judged
    const sense = port.*.status & DC_HPD_INT_STATUS__DC_HPD_SENSE != 0
    const delayed = port.*.status & DC_HPD_INT_STATUS__DC_HPD_SENSE_DELAYED != 0
    if sense != delayed { return Level.changing }
    return if sense { Level.high } else { Level.low }
}

// Every connector of the ROM looked at for the first time: its record
// made, what serves it read, its line read and taken as it is. A line
// that is changing is read again until it rests, BOOT_SETTLE at most
// (and BOOT_LOOKS times at most).
// `machine` and `board`: the ROM as gpu_rom.mlx read it (null: it was
// not).
pub fn survey(card: *const scanout.Card, machine: ?*const atom.Atom, board: ?*const videorom.Board, ports: *Ports) -> void {
    ports.*.found = false
    ports.*.count = 0
    ports.*.looks = 0
    ports.*.lookedAt = card.time()
    if machine == null || board == null || !board.?.*.found { return }
    const from = board.?
    var k: usize = 0
    while k < from.*.count && k < videorom.CONNECTORS {
        const port = &ports.*.ports[k]
        port.*.index = k
        port.*.rom = from.*.connectors[k]
        port.*.wire = ddc.wireOf(machine.?, &from.*.connectors[k])
        port.*.line = 0
        port.*.why = Why.judged
        if port.*.rom.hotPlug == videorom.NONE {
            port.*.why = Why.no_record
        } else if port.*.rom.hotPlugLine == 0 || port.*.rom.hotPlugLine > LINES {
            // (gpu_rom.mlx's "none of the six" is a number above them.)
            port.*.why = Why.not_placed
        } else {
            port.*.line = port.*.rom.hotPlugLine
        }
        port.*.status = 0
        port.*.control = 0
        port.*.filter = 0
        port.*.masked = false
        port.*.heard.clear()
        port.*.heardAt = 0
        port.*.monitor = edid.read(port.*.heard.data())
        port.*.offer = edid.nothing()
        port.*.askAt = 0
        port.*.asked = 0
        serve(card, port)
        if port.*.line != 0 { port.*.filter = card.reg(DC_HPD_TOGGLE_FILT_CNTL + (port.*.line - 1) * HPD_STEP) }
        const began = card.time()
        var level = readLine(card, port, card.reg(DC_GPIO_HPD_MASK))
        var again: usize = 0
        while level == Level.changing && card.time() - began < BOOT_SETTLE && again < BOOT_LOOKS {
            level = readLine(card, port, card.reg(DC_GPIO_HPD_MASK))
            again += 1
        }
        port.*.level = level
        port.*.since = card.time()
        port.*.atBoot = true
        port.*.seen = level
        port.*.seenSince = port.*.since
        port.*.seenLooks = AGREE
        k += 1
    }
    ports.*.count = k
    ports.*.found = k != 0
}

// How long a line's two sense bits may differ before the line counts as
// one that cannot be read: while a filter of the block's own runs they
// differ, so as long as the block's filter word (as the survey found
// it) can hold them apart by the longer of Linux's two readings of its
// delays, 10 ms a count, and a look more; and as long as at boot at
// least (Linux's own 50 counts and a look come to just that).
fn settle(port: *const Port) -> usize {
    const plug = port.*.filter & DC_HPD_TOGGLE_FILT_CNTL__DC_HPD_CONNECT_INT_DELAY
    const unplug = (port.*.filter & DC_HPD_TOGGLE_FILT_CNTL__DC_HPD_DISCONNECT_INT_DELAY) >> 20
    const own = (if plug > unplug { plug } else { unplug }) * 10000000 + INTERVAL
    return if own > BOOT_SETTLE { own } else { BOOT_SETTLE }
}

// How long a line has to stay at `level` before the change counts, in
// nanoseconds: Linux's filter for the connector's kind; for a line whose
// two sense bits differ, `settle`; what else cannot be read counts at
// once.
fn rest(port: *const Port, level: Level) -> usize {
    if level == Level.changing { return settle(port) }
    if level != Level.low && level != Level.high { return 0 }
    const tmds = port.tmdsKind()
    const milliseconds = if level == Level.high { if tmds { CONNECT_TMDS } else { CONNECT_DP } } else { if tmds { DISCONNECT_TMDS } else { DISCONNECT_DP } }
    return milliseconds * 1000000
}

// One look at every line. A change that the looks agree on is taken:
// the record gets the new level and what serves the connector now, and
// the change is put into `changes`.
pub fn look(card: *const scanout.Card, ports: *Ports, changes: *Changes) -> void {
    changes.*.count = 0
    if !ports.*.found { return }
    const now = card.time()
    ports.*.looks += 1
    ports.*.lookedAt = now
    const pins = card.reg(DC_GPIO_HPD_MASK)
    var k: usize = 0
    while k < ports.*.count {
        const port = &ports.*.ports[k]
        k += 1
        if port.*.line == 0 { continue }
        const level = readLine(card, port, pins)
        if level != port.*.seen {
            port.*.seen = level
            port.*.seenSince = now
            port.*.seenLooks = 1
        } else {
            port.*.seenLooks += 1
        }
        if level == port.*.level { continue }
        if port.*.seenLooks < AGREE || now - port.*.seenSince < rest(port, level) { continue }
        changes.*.list[changes.*.count] = Change.{ .port = port.*.index, .was = port.*.level, .now = level }
        changes.*.count += 1
        port.*.level = level
        port.*.since = port.*.seenSince
        port.*.atBoot = false
        serve(card, port)
    }
}

// What the detection found.
pub const Case = enum(u8) {
    // The ROM's connector records were not read or name none.
    no_records,
    // The firmware's output has a monitor, and no other connector has
    // anything.
    there,
    // It has one, and other connectors have something too.
    there_and_more,
    // It has none; another connector has something.
    elsewhere,
    // Its line cannot be judged.
    unjudged,
    // Nothing is plugged in anywhere (where a line can be read).
    nothing,
    // No picture goes out by a connector of the ROM's.
    not_started,
    // The firmware's picture goes out by several connectors.
    several_outputs
}

pub const Verdict = struct {
    what: Case
    // The connector the firmware's picture goes out by (the first, of
    // `outputs`; NONE: none), how many connectors have their line high,
    // and the first of those that the firmware did not start (NONE).
    output: usize
    outputs: usize
    plugged: usize
    other: usize
    // What the output sends: the path that follows.
    sends: Sends
}

pub fn detect(ports: *const Ports, verdict: *Verdict) -> void {
    verdict.* = Verdict.{ .what = Case.no_records, .output = NONE, .outputs = 0, .plugged = 0, .other = NONE, .sends = Sends.nothing }
    if !ports.*.found { return }
    var k: usize = 0
    while k < ports.*.count {
        const port = &ports.*.ports[k]
        if port.*.firmware {
            if verdict.*.output == NONE { verdict.*.output = k }
            verdict.*.outputs += 1
        }
        if port.*.level == Level.high {
            verdict.*.plugged += 1
            if !port.*.firmware && verdict.*.other == NONE { verdict.*.other = k }
        }
        k += 1
    }
    if verdict.*.outputs == 0 {
        verdict.*.what = if verdict.*.plugged != 0 { Case.not_started } else { Case.nothing }
        return
    }
    const output = &ports.*.ports[verdict.*.output]
    verdict.*.sends = output.*.sends
    if verdict.*.outputs > 1 {
        verdict.*.what = Case.several_outputs
    } else if output.*.level == Level.high {
        verdict.*.what = if verdict.*.other != NONE { Case.there_and_more } else { Case.there }
    } else if output.*.level == Level.low {
        verdict.*.what = if verdict.*.other != NONE { Case.elsewhere } else { Case.nothing }
    } else {
        verdict.*.what = Case.unjudged
    }
}

// The connector that `controller`'s picture goes out by when its hot
// plug line says for certain that nothing is plugged in there (and no
// other connector the same controller serves has something); NONE
// otherwise: when the line is high, cannot be judged, or no connector of
// the ROM's is served by the controller.
pub fn emptyFor(ports: *const Ports, controller: usize) -> usize {
    var empty: usize = NONE
    var k: usize = 0
    while k < ports.*.count {
        const port = &ports.*.ports[k]
        if port.*.firmware && port.*.controller == controller {
            if port.*.level != Level.low { return NONE }
            if empty == NONE { empty = k }
        }
        k += 1
    }
    return empty
}

// The first connector `controller`'s picture goes out by (NONE: none of
// the ROM's).
pub fn servedBy(ports: *const Ports, controller: usize) -> usize {
    var k: usize = 0
    while k < ports.*.count {
        if ports.*.ports[k].firmware && ports.*.ports[k].controller == controller { return k }
        k += 1
    }
    return NONE
}

// How many connectors' lines cannot be read as low or high.
fn unread(ports: *const Ports) -> usize {
    var count: usize = 0
    var k: usize = 0
    while k < ports.*.count {
        if !ports.*.ports[k].judged() { count += 1 }
        k += 1
    }
    return count
}

// "connector 2 (HDMI)".
pub fn sayName(card: *const scanout.Card, port: *const Port) -> void {
    card.field("connector ", port.*.index)
    card.say(" (")
    card.say(videorom.connectorName(port.*.rom.kind))
    card.say(")")
}

// "(DIG 4, hot plug line 2)", "(DIG 4, no hot plug line)".
fn sayPlace(card: *const scanout.Card, port: *const Port) -> void {
    if port.*.rom.transmitter != videorom.NONE {
        card.field("DIG ", port.*.rom.transmitter)
    } else {
        card.say("no transmitter of the card's own")
    }
    if port.*.line != 0 {
        card.field(", hot plug line ", port.*.line)
    } else {
        card.say(", no hot plug line")
    }
}

// "the HDMI connector 2 (DIG 4, hot plug line 2)".
fn sayWhere(card: *const scanout.Card, port: *const Port) -> void {
    card.say("the ")
    card.say(videorom.connectorName(port.*.rom.kind))
    card.field(" connector ", port.*.index)
    card.say(" (")
    sayPlace(card, port)
    card.say(")")
}

fn whyText(port: *const Port) -> []const u8 {
    return match port.*.why {
        .judged => "its two sense bits differ",
        .no_record => "the ROM names no hot plug pin for it",
        .not_placed => "its hot plug pin is none of the card's six lines",
        .no_answer => "the line's block does not answer",
        .block_off => "the line's block is off, and Linux's display core counts on the firmware having left it on",
        .pin_masked => "the line's pin is held as a plain pin, and Linux's display core gives it to the hot plug block before it looks"
    }
}

// One connector as it was found: "gpu: connector 2 (HDMI, DIG 4, hot
// plug line 2): a monitor is plugged in; the firmware's picture goes out
// here".
pub fn sayPort(card: *const scanout.Card, port: *const Port) -> void {
    card.field("gpu: connector ", port.*.index)
    card.say(" (")
    card.say(videorom.connectorName(port.*.rom.kind))
    card.say(", ")
    sayPlace(card, port)
    card.say("): ")
    if port.*.level == Level.high {
        card.say(if port.*.firmware { "a monitor is plugged in; the firmware's picture goes out here" } else { "something is plugged in; the firmware did not start this output" })
    } else if port.*.level == Level.low {
        card.say(if port.*.firmware { "nothing is plugged in, yet the firmware's picture goes out here" } else { "nothing is plugged in" })
    } else {
        card.say(if port.*.level == Level.no_line { "cannot be looked at: " } else if port.*.level == Level.changing { "the line is changing: " } else { "whether something is plugged in is not known: " })
        card.say(whyText(port))
        if port.*.firmware { card.say("; the firmware's picture goes out here") }
    }
    card.say("\n")
}

// Every connector's line, then each line's words (for the log alone:
// the caller keeps them off the screen).
pub fn sayPorts(card: *const scanout.Card, ports: *const Ports) -> void {
    var k: usize = 0
    while k < ports.*.count {
        sayPort(card, &ports.*.ports[k])
        k += 1
    }
}

pub fn sayWords(card: *const scanout.Card, ports: *const Ports) -> void {
    var k: usize = 0
    while k < ports.*.count {
        const port = &ports.*.ports[k]
        k += 1
        if port.*.line == 0 { continue }
        card.field("gpu: hot plug line ", port.*.line)
        card.fieldHex(": status ", port.*.status)
        card.fieldHex(", control ", port.*.control)
        card.fieldHex(", filter ", port.*.filter)
        // (The two delays as the word holds them: Linux's two drivers
        // write the same numbers and mean times ten apart by them, so
        // no unit is said.)
        card.field(" (its delay for a plug ", port.*.filter & DC_HPD_TOGGLE_FILT_CNTL__DC_HPD_CONNECT_INT_DELAY)
        card.field(", for an unplug ", (port.*.filter & DC_HPD_TOGGLE_FILT_CNTL__DC_HPD_DISCONNECT_INT_DELAY) >> 20)
        card.say(if port.*.masked { "); its pin is held as a plain pin\n" } else { "); its pin is the block's\n" })
    }
}

fn pathText(sends: Sends) -> []const u8 {
    return match sends {
        .nothing => "nothing is sent",
        .tmds => "the TMDS path",
        .displayport => "the DisplayPort path",
        .other => "a signal this driver has no path for"
    }
}

// The connectors that have something plugged in and that the firmware
// did not start, each in a line.
fn sayOthers(card: *const scanout.Card, ports: *const Ports, also: bool) -> void {
    var k: usize = 0
    while k < ports.*.count {
        const port = &ports.*.ports[k]
        if port.*.level == Level.high && !port.*.firmware {
            card.say("gpu: something is plugged into ")
            sayName(card, port)
            card.say(if also { " too, which the firmware did not start: this driver does not bring it up yet\n" } else { ", which the firmware did not start: this driver does not bring it up yet\n" })
        }
        k += 1
    }
}

// What was detected, and which path follows: "gpu: the monitor is on the
// HDMI connector 2 (DIG 4, hot plug line 2): the TMDS path".
pub fn sayVerdict(card: *const scanout.Card, ports: *const Ports, verdict: *const Verdict) -> void {
    if verdict.*.what == Case.no_records {
        card.say("gpu: the card's ROM names no connectors (or was not read): where the monitor is cannot be looked for\n")
        return
    }
    if verdict.*.what == Case.not_started {
        sayOthers(card, ports, false)
        card.say("gpu: the firmware's picture goes out by none of the ROM's connectors: there is no output of this card to set a mode on\n")
        return
    }
    if verdict.*.output == NONE {
        card.say(if unread(ports) == 0 { "gpu: nothing is plugged into any connector of the card" } else { "gpu: nothing is plugged into any connector whose hot plug line can be read" })
        card.say(", and the firmware's picture goes out by none of them\n")
        return
    }
    const output = &ports.*.ports[verdict.*.output]
    if verdict.*.what == Case.several_outputs {
        card.field("gpu: the firmware's picture goes out by ", verdict.*.outputs)
        card.say(" connectors:")
        var k: usize = 0
        while k < ports.*.count {
            const port = &ports.*.ports[k]
            if port.*.firmware {
                card.say(" ")
                sayName(card, port)
                card.say(if port.*.level == Level.high { ", a monitor is plugged in;" } else if port.*.level == Level.low { ", nothing is plugged in;" } else { ", not known whether something is plugged in;" })
            }
            k += 1
        }
        card.say(" nobody chooses between them here\n")
        sayOthers(card, ports, true)
        return
    }
    if verdict.*.what == Case.there || verdict.*.what == Case.there_and_more {
        card.say("gpu: the monitor is on ")
        sayWhere(card, output)
        if output.*.rom.kind == videorom.CONNECTOR_OBJECT_ID_DISPLAYPORT && output.*.sends == Sends.tmds { card.say(", which sends TMDS (an adapter is behind it)") }
        card.say(": ")
        card.say(pathText(output.*.sends))
        card.say("\n")
        sayOthers(card, ports, true)
        return
    }
    if verdict.*.what == Case.elsewhere {
        card.say("gpu: nothing is plugged into ")
        sayWhere(card, output)
        card.say(", where the firmware's picture goes out\n")
        sayOthers(card, ports, false)
        return
    }
    if verdict.*.what == Case.nothing {
        card.say(if unread(ports) == 0 { "gpu: nothing is plugged into any connector of the card" } else { "gpu: nothing is plugged into any connector whose hot plug line can be read" })
        card.say("; the firmware's picture goes out by ")
        sayName(card, output)
        card.say(" all the same\n")
        return
    }
    card.say("gpu: the firmware's picture goes out by ")
    sayWhere(card, output)
    card.say(if output.*.level == Level.no_line { ", which has no hot plug line to say whether a monitor is there: " } else { ", whose hot plug line does not say whether a monitor is there: " })
    card.say(pathText(output.*.sends))
    card.say(", as the firmware left it\n")
    sayOthers(card, ports, true)
}

// A change, once: "gpu: the monitor was unplugged from connector 2
// (HDMI)", "gpu: something was plugged into connector 3 (DisplayPort)".
pub fn sayChange(card: *const scanout.Card, ports: *const Ports, change: *const Change) -> void {
    const port = &ports.*.ports[change.*.port]
    if (change.*.was == Level.not_known || change.*.was == Level.changing) && (change.*.now == Level.high || change.*.now == Level.low) {
        card.say("gpu: ")
        sayName(card, port)
        card.say(if change.*.now == Level.high { ": its hot plug line can be read now: something is plugged in\n" } else { ": its hot plug line can be read now: nothing is plugged in\n" })
    } else if change.*.now == Level.high {
        card.say("gpu: something was plugged into ")
        sayName(card, port)
        card.say(if port.*.firmware { ", where the picture goes out\n" } else { "\n" })
    } else if change.*.now == Level.low {
        const known = port.*.firmware || port.*.heard.outcome == ddc.Outcome.read
        card.say(if known { "gpu: the monitor was unplugged from " } else { "gpu: what was plugged in was unplugged from " })
        sayName(card, port)
        card.say(if port.*.firmware { ": the picture still goes out there, to nothing\n" } else { "\n" })
    } else {
        card.say("gpu: ")
        sayName(card, port)
        card.say(": its hot plug line cannot be read any more: ")
        card.say(whyText(port))
        card.say("\n")
    }
}

// "DDC line 0, the card's DDC1": the line by the number the ROM has for
// it, which is what the connector's own line of the video BIOS says
// ("gpu: connector 2: HDMI (...; DDC line 0)"), and by the card's name
// for the pins and words it is read by (DDC1 to DDC6): the two numbers
// are not the same thing, and one name for both would read as two lines.
fn sayLine(card: *const scanout.Card, port: *const Port) -> void {
    card.field("DDC line ", port.*.wire.entry)
    card.field(", the card's DDC", port.*.heard.line + 1)
}

fn wireText(why: ddc.Unwired) -> []const u8 {
    return match why {
        .wired => "its DDC line is known",
        .no_record => "the ROM names no DDC line for it",
        .no_table => "the ROM's table of DDC lines is not there or not one Linux reads",
        .beyond_table => "the ROM names a DDC line its table of lines does not have",
        .not_a_line => "the ROM's DDC line for it is none of the card's six"
    }
}

// What a monitor said, or why it was not heard: "gpu: connector 3
// (HDMI): the monitor says 3840x1080 at 60 Hz, HDMI, up to 300 MHz (EDID
// of 2 blocks over DDC line 2, the card's DDC3)".
pub fn sayHeard(card: *const scanout.Card, port: *const Port) -> void {
    const heard = &port.*.heard
    if heard.*.outcome == ddc.Outcome.not_asked { return }
    card.say("gpu: ")
    sayName(card, port)
    card.say(": ")
    if heard.*.outcome == ddc.Outcome.read {
        if port.*.monitor.state == edid.State.found {
            card.size("the monitor says ", port.*.monitor.width, port.*.monitor.height)
            card.field(" at ", (port.*.monitor.timing.millihertz() + 500) / 1000)
            card.say(" Hz")
            if port.*.offer.hdmi {
                card.say(", HDMI")
                if port.*.offer.limit != 0 {
                    card.field(", up to ", port.*.offer.limit / 1000)
                    card.say(" MHz")
                }
            } else {
                card.say(", DVI (its EDID has no HDMI block)")
            }
        } else {
            card.say("a monitor answers, and its EDID names no timing of its own that can be used")
        }
        card.field(" (EDID of ", heard.*.blocks)
        card.say(if heard.*.blocks == 1 { " block over " } else { " blocks over " })
        sayLine(card, port)
        if !heard.*.complete {
            card.field("; it names ", heard.*.extensions + 1)
            card.say(": the others were not read or do not hold")
        }
        card.say(")\n")
    } else if heard.*.outcome == ddc.Outcome.no_answer {
        card.say("nothing answers on ")
        sayLine(card, port)
        card.say(": no EDID (a monitor that is off, or none)\n")
    } else if heard.*.outcome == ddc.Outcome.does_not_hold {
        card.say("what answers on ")
        sayLine(card, port)
        card.say(", gives no EDID that holds (")
        if heard.*.sum == 0 {
            card.say("its first bytes are not an EDID's")
        } else {
            card.fieldHex("its base block adds up to ", heard.*.sum)
        }
        card.say(")\n")
    } else if heard.*.failure == ddc.Failure.no_line {
        card.say("its monitor cannot be asked: ")
        card.say(wireText(port.*.wire.why))
        card.say("\n")
    } else {
        card.say("its EDID could not be read")
        if heard.*.line != ddc.NONE {
            card.say(" over ")
            sayLine(card, port)
        }
        card.say(": ")
        card.say(ddc.failureText(heard.*.failure))
        card.fieldHex(" (the engine's status ", heard.*.status)
        card.say(")\n")
    }
    if !heard.*.restored {
        card.say("gpu: ")
        sayLine(card, port)
        card.fieldHex(": the register ", heard.*.stuck)
        card.say(" did not take back what it held before the EDID was read\n")
    }
}

// A reading that is over put into the connector's record.
fn keep(card: *const scanout.Card, port: *Port, reader: *const ddc.Reader) -> void {
    port.*.heard = reader.*.heard
    port.*.heardAt = card.time()
    port.*.monitor = edid.read(port.*.heard.data())
    port.*.offer = edid.offer(port.*.heard.data())
}

// Whether a connector's monitor is asked at boot: one that sends or
// would send TMDS; the firmware's output unless its line says for
// certain that nothing is there, another connector when its line is
// high.
pub fn askedAtBoot(port: *const Port) -> bool {
    if !port.asks() { return false }
    return if port.*.firmware { port.*.level != Level.low } else { port.*.level == Level.high }
}

// The monitors asked at boot, one after another, each said: `hands`
// writes the card's I2C engine, `pause` waits (microseconds), `lines`
// and `crystal` as gpu_ddc.mlx's `read` takes them.
pub fn askAtBoot(card: *const scanout.Card, hands: *const ddc.Hands, pause: fn(usize) -> void, lines: usize, crystal: usize, ports: *Ports, reader: *ddc.Reader) -> void {
    var k: usize = 0
    while k < ports.*.count {
        const port = &ports.*.ports[k]
        k += 1
        if !askedAtBoot(port) { continue }
        ddc.read(card, hands, pause, &port.*.wire, lines, crystal, reader)
        port.*.asked = 1
        keep(card, port, reader)
        sayHeard(card, port)
    }
}

// How an EDID read from a connector stands to the one the loader passed
// on.
pub const Against = enum(u8) {
    // The loader passed none on.
    no_other,
    // The same bytes, as far as both go.
    same,
    // They differ (`at`: the first byte that does).
    differs
}

pub fn against(heard: *const ddc.Heard, loader: []const u8, at: *usize) -> Against {
    at.* = 0
    if loader.length < edid.BLOCK { return Against.no_other }
    const common = if loader.length < heard.*.count { loader.length } else { heard.*.count }
    var k: usize = 0
    while k < common {
        if loader[k] != heard.*.bytes[k] {
            at.* = k
            return Against.differs
        }
        k += 1
    }
    return Against.same
}

// Which EDID the plan for the firmware's output is made from: the one
// the driver read from the connector, or the one the EFI loader got
// from the firmware.
pub const Counts = enum(u8) {
    // Nothing that holds was read from the connector, and the loader
    // passed none on.
    nothing,
    // Nothing that holds was read from the connector: the loader's.
    loaders,
    // The same bytes, and as many: the connector's (it is the same).
    same,
    // The same as far as both go, and the loader's has more: the
    // loader's.
    loaders_longer,
    // The same as far as both go, and the connector's has more (a
    // firmware may hand the loader the first block alone, and what says
    // that a monitor is an HDMI one is in the second): the connector's.
    connectors_longer,
    // Another EDID than the loader's, read whole: the connector's (it is
    // the monitor that is there now, and all Linux ever reads).
    connectors_other,
    // Another one, and not read whole: the loader's.
    loaders_other,
    // The loader passed none on: the connector's, as far as it was read.
    connectors_alone
}

pub fn counts(heard: *const ddc.Heard, loader: []const u8, at: *usize) -> Counts {
    at.* = 0
    const other = loader.length >= edid.BLOCK
    if heard.*.outcome != ddc.Outcome.read { return if other { Counts.loaders } else { Counts.nothing } }
    const stands = against(heard, loader, at)
    if stands == Against.no_other { return Counts.connectors_alone }
    if stands == Against.differs { return if heard.*.complete { Counts.connectors_other } else { Counts.loaders_other } }
    if heard.*.count < loader.length { return Counts.loaders_longer }
    return if heard.*.count > loader.length { Counts.connectors_longer } else { Counts.same }
}

// Whether the plan is made from the EDID read from the connector.
pub fn fromConnector(which: Counts) -> bool { return which == Counts.same || which == Counts.connectors_longer || which == Counts.connectors_other || which == Counts.connectors_alone }

// Which one counts, said: "gpu: connector 2 (HDMI): the loader passed on
// the first 1 block of it alone: the plan goes by all 2, read from the
// connector now". `loaderBytes`: how many bytes the loader passed on.
pub fn sayCounts(card: *const scanout.Card, port: *const Port, which: Counts, at: usize, loaderBytes: usize) -> void {
    if which == Counts.nothing { return }
    card.say("gpu: ")
    sayName(card, port)
    if which == Counts.loaders {
        card.say(": the EDID the loader passed on counts\n")
    } else if which == Counts.same {
        card.say(": it is the EDID the loader passed on\n")
    } else if which == Counts.loaders_longer {
        card.say(": it is the EDID the loader passed on as far as it was read: the loader's, which has more of it, counts\n")
    } else if which == Counts.connectors_longer {
        card.field(": the loader passed on the first ", loaderBytes / edid.BLOCK)
        card.field(if loaderBytes / edid.BLOCK == 1 { " block of it alone: the plan goes by all " } else { " blocks of it alone: the plan goes by all " }, port.*.heard.blocks)
        card.say(", read from the connector now\n")
    } else if which == Counts.connectors_alone {
        card.say(": the loader passed no EDID on: the one read from the connector counts\n")
    } else {
        card.field(": the EDID read from the connector differs from the one the loader passed on (from byte ", at)
        card.say(if which == Counts.connectors_other { " on): the one read now counts, it is the monitor that is there\n" } else { " on) and was not read whole: the loader's counts\n" })
    }
}

// The looking and the asking while brixOS runs.
pub const Watch = struct {
    ports: Ports
    reader: ddc.Reader
    // The connector whose EDID is being read (NONE: none is), and when
    // the next look is due.
    reading: usize
    next: usize
    lines: usize
    crystal: usize
    changes: Changes

    pub fn begin(self: *Self, card: *const scanout.Card, lines: usize, crystal: usize) -> void {
        self.*.reader.idle()
        self.*.reading = NONE
        self.*.next = card.time() + INTERVAL
        self.*.lines = lines
        self.*.crystal = crystal
        self.*.changes.count = 0
    }
}

// A tick of the timer: the reading of an EDID taken a piece further;
// when a look is due, the lines looked at and each change said; then,
// when no EDID is being read, the next monitor that is to be asked.
// `hands`: null at a boot that keeps the firmware's picture (nobody is
// asked then). What changed at this tick is in `watch.changes`.
pub fn tick(card: *const scanout.Card, hands: ?*const ddc.Hands, watch: *Watch) -> void {
    watch.*.changes.count = 0
    const now = card.time()
    if watch.*.reading != NONE && hands != null {
        var pieces: usize = 0
        var next = ddc.Next.again
        while next == ddc.Next.again && pieces < PIECES {
            next = watch.*.reader.step(card, hands.?, false)
            pieces += 1
        }
        if next == ddc.Next.done {
            const port = &watch.*.ports.ports[watch.*.reading]
            watch.*.reading = NONE
            const failed = watch.*.reader.heard.outcome != ddc.Outcome.read
            // (Unplugged meanwhile: what the reading gave is nothing's.
            // Unplugged and plugged in again meanwhile: it may be the
            // monitor's that is gone, in part or whole; what is there
            // now is asked for itself, as its plug has put down.)
            if port.*.level == Level.high && port.*.since <= watch.*.reader.began {
                if failed && port.*.asked < 2 {
                    // (Once more, a second later.)
                    port.*.askAt = now + ASK_AGAIN
                } else {
                    keep(card, port, &watch.*.reader)
                    sayHeard(card, port)
                }
            }
        }
    }
    if now >= watch.*.next {
        watch.*.next = now + INTERVAL
        look(card, &watch.*.ports, &watch.*.changes)
        var k: usize = 0
        while k < watch.*.changes.count {
            const change = &watch.*.changes.list[k]
            const port = &watch.*.ports.ports[change.*.port]
            sayChange(card, &watch.*.ports, change)
            if change.*.now == Level.high && port.asks() {
                port.*.askAt = now
                port.*.asked = 0
            } else if change.*.now != Level.high {
                // (What was heard was the monitor that is gone.)
                port.*.askAt = 0
                port.*.heard.clear()
                port.*.monitor = edid.read(port.*.heard.data())
                port.*.offer = edid.nothing()
            }
            k += 1
        }
    }
    if watch.*.reading != NONE || hands == null { return }
    var k: usize = 0
    while k < watch.*.ports.count {
        const port = &watch.*.ports.ports[k]
        if port.*.askAt != 0 && now >= port.*.askAt && port.*.level == Level.high {
            port.*.askAt = 0
            port.*.asked += 1
            watch.*.reader.start(card, &port.*.wire, watch.*.lines, watch.*.crystal)
            watch.*.reading = k
            return
        }
        k += 1
    }
}
